    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="0380" n="368" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg368"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/3821: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-15T16:00:00+03:00">June 15, 1940—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 15—3:07 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>681. I have just been informed by the Associated Press correspondent that an
                    official communiqué was issued a few minutes ago by the Soviet Government
                    stating that an ultimatum addressed by it to the Lithuanian Government had been
                    accepted by the latter this morning at 9 o’clock, one hour before its time limit
                    expired.</p>
                <p>The communiqué which is not yet available to the Embassy is somewhat lengthy. I
                    understand that its principal points are that the former Lithuanian Minister of
                        Interior<anchor n="26" xml:id="d362-f1"/> and head of the Political Police
                        Department<anchor n="27" xml:id="d362-f2"/> must be brought to trial in
                    connection with the Soviet protest, (Embassy’s telegram 601, May 29, 12
                    midnight) that the number of Soviet troops in Lithuania must be increased and
                    that the Lithuanian Government must be reorganized.</p>
                <p>Repeated [to] Kaunas and Riga.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d362-f1" n="26" xml:id="d362-f1-content">
                <p>K. Skucas, removed from his office on June 13, 1940.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d362-f2" n="27" xml:id="d362-f2-content">
                <p>A. Povilaitis, Director of the Department of State Security since 1932, removed
                    from his office on June 13, 1940.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
